00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:48.439
friend crushes, you know?

00:01:48.439 --> 00:01:49.272
- Well, I didn't know that counted.

00:01:49.272 --> 00:01:50.670
- I Think you guys have been so diplomatic

00:01:50.670 --> 00:01:52.020
by saying, "friend crush."

00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:52.853
- Yeah.

00:01:52.853 --> 00:01:54.180
- Come on. We don't wanna
know about friend crush.

00:01:54.180 --> 00:01:55.830
We wanna know about actual crush.

00:01:56.700 --> 00:01:58.950
- Feelings of desire
can kind of be ambiguous

00:01:58.950 --> 00:02:00.240
of whether or not it's actually...

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:02.010
If it's platonic or if it's like,

00:02:02.010 --> 00:02:02.910
something more than that.

00:02:02.910 --> 00:02:04.260
- I think...

00:02:04.260 --> 00:02:06.180
I'm a 23 year old like,

00:02:06.180 --> 00:02:08.670
single woman in LA.

00:02:08.670 --> 00:02:11.520
I'm just checking out
everyone all the time.

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:13.680
I love to check people out and you know,

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:16.590
the qualities of the people
that work at Jubilee like,

00:02:16.590 --> 00:02:18.570
a lot of emotionally intelligent people.

00:02:18.570 --> 00:02:20.010
A lot of sensitive, soft people.

00:02:20.010 --> 00:02:21.870
These are like my
favorite types of people.

00:02:21.870 --> 00:02:24.813
So like, I feel like in every
single person at Jubilee,

00:02:25.650 --> 00:02:27.210
I would kiss them for a different reason.

00:02:27.210 --> 00:02:28.043
You know?

00:02:28.043 --> 00:02:29.850
Like, they all have qualities that...

00:02:29.850 --> 00:02:30.750
You don't like that, Steven?

00:02:30.750 --> 00:02:32.820
You don't like the way I said that?

00:02:32.820 --> 00:02:34.290
- I mean...

00:02:34.290 --> 00:02:35.123
I wouldn't kiss everyone.

00:02:35.123 --> 00:02:36.383
- It doesn't have to be with Tina.

00:02:37.710 --> 00:02:40.193
You're the one who said I
don't have a crush on Stephen.

00:02:42.780 --> 00:02:44.913
- [Director] I love reading the comments.

00:02:45.750 --> 00:02:48.120
Three, two, one.

00:02:55.800 --> 00:02:58.530
- No, I don't bother with
the comments section.

00:02:58.530 --> 00:02:59.730
I mean, sometimes they're funny.

00:02:59.730 --> 00:03:02.340
But sometimes people are just mean

00:03:02.340 --> 00:03:05.730
and it makes me lose faith
in humanity a little bit

00:03:05.730 --> 00:03:06.780
to see so much like,

00:03:06.780 --> 00:03:08.640
very specific cruelty.

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:10.440
- I kind of agree with that.

00:03:10.440 --> 00:03:14.010
That sometimes the comments
can be pretty brutal and mean.

00:03:14.010 --> 00:03:14.880
But I think as a director,

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:17.070
sometimes I get pretty
good feedback from comments

00:03:17.070 --> 00:03:19.800
because people have good
suggestions about different topics

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:22.350
on how the video could have been better

00:03:22.350 --> 00:03:24.510
with maybe a few different
prompts or different cast.

00:03:24.510 --> 00:03:27.180
So it's not that I take
everything seriously,

00:03:27.180 --> 00:03:29.490
but it's like one of my
guilty pleasures also.

00:03:29.490 --> 00:03:32.580
- I think it's also just
a really good way to see

00:03:32.580 --> 00:03:34.860
if the content that we're
even making even resonates

00:03:34.860 --> 00:03:35.730
with our audience.

00:03:35.730 --> 00:03:37.011
- But, yeah.

00:03:37.011 --> 00:03:37.844
Like, if we didn't like,
look at the comments,

00:03:37.844 --> 00:03:38.677
we'd probably just like,

00:03:38.677 --> 00:03:40.560
evaluate our videos by the views.

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:42.120
Which there are so many videos that like,

00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:43.740
don't get a lot of views but are like,

00:03:43.740 --> 00:03:45.600
some of the videos that
we're most proud of.

00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:47.153
- Yeah. I'm on the same wavelength.

00:03:47.153 --> 00:03:50.610
I think we create content
for our audiences so like,

00:03:50.610 --> 00:03:52.680
it's always good to know what
we think we're doing right

00:03:52.680 --> 00:03:54.540
and what we may be doing wrong.

00:03:54.540 --> 00:03:57.870
And because I have been in
like a few little videos.

00:03:57.870 --> 00:03:59.820
Brandon showed me this one website

00:03:59.820 --> 00:04:01.380
where you could search up like,

00:04:01.380 --> 00:04:02.820
specific topics.

00:04:02.820 --> 00:04:04.590
So I always look for my name.

00:04:04.590 --> 00:04:05.753
Because I wanna know what
people think about me!

00:04:05.753 --> 00:04:07.170
I wanna be liked.

00:04:07.170 --> 00:04:08.580
- Going off of Milky's point.

00:04:08.580 --> 00:04:10.380
I can definitely attest to like,

00:04:10.380 --> 00:04:12.690
checking the comments to make
sure we're doing a good job

00:04:12.690 --> 00:04:13.523
As an editor,

00:04:13.523 --> 00:04:14.850
I put a lot of jokes in

00:04:14.850 --> 00:04:17.730
that sometimes I'm worried will
go over the audience's head

00:04:17.730 --> 00:04:18.563
or like,

00:04:18.563 --> 00:04:19.920
they just won't get it.

00:04:19.920 --> 00:04:21.510
My humor is different from
that of the audience's.

00:04:21.510 --> 00:04:22.380
So it's a process of like,

00:04:22.380 --> 00:04:26.070
learning what the audience
likes in terms of jokes.

00:04:26.070 --> 00:04:27.540
Also making sure we, you know,

00:04:27.540 --> 00:04:29.580
are hitting our targets in terms of like,

00:04:29.580 --> 00:04:30.630
what the video is about.

00:04:30.630 --> 00:04:32.880
Are we engaging our
community in the right way?

00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:34.680
There are also instances of like,

00:04:34.680 --> 00:04:36.820
people doxxing people in the comments

00:04:36.820 --> 00:04:39.930
that I think we do have a
responsibility to look out for.

00:04:39.930 --> 00:04:42.030
- And our channel is left leaning.

00:04:42.030 --> 00:04:45.180
So, a lot of people who have opinions

00:04:45.180 --> 00:04:46.013
that are right leaning,

00:04:46.013 --> 00:04:47.580
they definitely are under attack.

00:04:47.580 --> 00:04:48.900
For so many videos.

00:04:48.900 --> 00:04:50.880
- I think we have to be
really cognizant of like,

00:04:50.880 --> 00:04:52.530
tunnel vision and just like,

00:04:52.530 --> 00:04:55.890
the echo chamber of the same
voices saying the same thing.

00:04:55.890 --> 00:04:57.000
- Yeah.

00:04:57.000 --> 00:04:59.820
- [Director] No topic or
identity should be off limits

00:04:59.820 --> 00:05:00.843
on our platform.

00:05:02.040 --> 00:05:04.380
Three, two, one.

00:05:10.527 --> 00:05:11.790
- Part of what like,

00:05:11.790 --> 00:05:12.810
why I fell in love with Jubilee

00:05:12.810 --> 00:05:14.160
and why I like working here is

00:05:14.160 --> 00:05:17.040
because we do take the
time to show both sides.

00:05:17.040 --> 00:05:18.990
Even if it is stuff or from people

00:05:18.990 --> 00:05:20.430
that I normally wouldn't agree with.

00:05:20.430 --> 00:05:24.840
Because if I don't know
how the other side thinks

00:05:24.840 --> 00:05:26.100
and I don't know like,

00:05:26.100 --> 00:05:27.720
what is causing them pain.

00:05:27.720 --> 00:05:28.830
Like, I don't know how to win them over.

00:05:28.830 --> 00:05:30.360
And if I don't know how to win them over,

00:05:30.360 --> 00:05:31.410
Then...

00:05:31.410 --> 00:05:32.760
Like, I can't hope for a better world.

00:05:32.760 --> 00:05:34.350
Like, changing minds is important.

00:05:34.350 --> 00:05:35.610
- I do think like,

00:05:35.610 --> 00:05:38.490
our whole mission is to
help create understanding

00:05:38.490 --> 00:05:39.960
on different points of views.

00:05:39.960 --> 00:05:42.120
But there are definitely
some points of views

00:05:42.120 --> 00:05:44.520
that I feel like are overly negative.

00:05:44.520 --> 00:05:46.740
Things that could be related to...

00:05:46.740 --> 00:05:50.400
Hate crime provoking conversation.

00:05:50.400 --> 00:05:53.190
- But that also depends on
how we showcase that content,

00:05:53.190 --> 00:05:54.023
right?

00:05:54.023 --> 00:05:54.856
Like, how we represent them.

00:05:54.856 --> 00:05:56.970
I think we should cover all topics

00:05:56.970 --> 00:05:58.230
and there's nothing off limits.

00:05:58.230 --> 00:05:59.063
Because at the end of the day,

00:05:59.063 --> 00:06:00.150
I do think like,

00:06:00.150 --> 00:06:01.800
nobody's born good or bad.

00:06:01.800 --> 00:06:03.960
It's the choices that
defines who they are.

00:06:03.960 --> 00:06:06.300
I think only if we try to explore topics

00:06:06.300 --> 00:06:07.890
that make us uncomfortable,

00:06:07.890 --> 00:06:11.130
can we create less divide amongst people?

00:06:11.130 --> 00:06:11.990
Of course that would be a lot

00:06:11.990 --> 00:06:14.730
of responsibility for us
as a company to make sure

00:06:14.730 --> 00:06:18.270
that the opinions of the
people are their opinions,

00:06:18.270 --> 00:06:20.940
and Jubilee doesn't
endorse anything like that.

00:06:20.940 --> 00:06:21.773
I think it's fine.

00:06:21.773 --> 00:06:25.500
- I think just that a lot of
misinformation can be spread.

00:06:25.500 --> 00:06:27.780
And I know that we are
very popular on like,

00:06:27.780 --> 00:06:29.310
platforms like Facebook.

00:06:29.310 --> 00:06:33.060
And a lot of older people may
get their news from Facebook

00:06:33.060 --> 00:06:35.130
and they may like,

00:06:35.130 --> 00:06:36.870
cherry pick this clip,

00:06:36.870 --> 00:06:37.703
this moment.

00:06:37.703 --> 00:06:38.536
And like,

00:06:38.536 --> 00:06:40.200
that could be incredibly influential

00:06:40.200 --> 00:06:41.683
to a huge amount of people.

00:06:41.683 --> 00:06:43.290
- I think it's important to remember also

00:06:43.290 --> 00:06:45.990
that platforming is not endorsing.

00:06:45.990 --> 00:06:46.950
You know.

00:06:46.950 --> 00:06:48.630
Just because we put
something on out platform,

00:06:48.630 --> 00:06:49.920
doesn't mean we agree with it.

00:06:49.920 --> 00:06:52.470
That's why we have something
like a verbal disclaimer

00:06:52.470 --> 00:06:54.090
on middle ground now.

00:06:54.090 --> 00:06:55.350
Yeah. Come on over.

00:06:55.350 --> 00:06:56.220
- But I think overall,

00:06:56.220 --> 00:06:59.340
this is maybe just a larger
conversation about censorship

00:06:59.340 --> 00:07:00.173
and like,

00:07:00.173 --> 00:07:02.457
what we believe should
be said and not said.

00:07:02.457 --> 00:07:05.010
And I think that's what a lot
of private companies are kind

00:07:05.010 --> 00:07:06.390
of considering now too.

00:07:06.390 --> 00:07:07.380
Which is why like,

00:07:07.380 --> 00:07:08.213
you know,

00:07:08.213 --> 00:07:11.190
Twitter banned Trump
and same with Facebook.

00:07:11.190 --> 00:07:13.950
I wonder if these big
corporations are doing

00:07:13.950 --> 00:07:17.490
that to protect the
overall good of people.

00:07:17.490 --> 00:07:20.610
I feel like we should in a way follow suit

00:07:20.610 --> 00:07:23.040
and be really considerate
of the people that would...

00:07:23.040 --> 00:07:25.470
Our audiences that would
be watching our videos.

00:07:25.470 --> 00:07:27.570
- I feel like I could honestly be

00:07:27.570 --> 00:07:29.760
on that side with you both.

00:07:29.760 --> 00:07:32.460
But I'm coming at it more from
like the business perspective

00:07:32.460 --> 00:07:33.293
of the fact that like,

00:07:33.293 --> 00:07:35.040
our content lives on YouTube.

00:07:35.040 --> 00:07:35.970
And we are kind of like,

00:07:35.970 --> 00:07:39.030
at the mercy of content
guidelines of YouTube.

00:07:39.030 --> 00:07:40.050
So like,

00:07:40.050 --> 00:07:42.780
if there's some sort
of topic or perspective

00:07:42.780 --> 00:07:44.130
that is gonna get us like,

00:07:44.130 --> 00:07:44.963
flagged.

00:07:44.963 --> 00:07:45.796
Get demonetized.

00:07:45.796 --> 00:07:46.980
That's messing with the livelihoods

00:07:46.980 --> 00:07:48.840
of all the people that
work for this company.

00:07:48.840 --> 00:07:50.460
That's where I'm moreso coming from

00:07:50.460 --> 00:07:53.523
If it's moreso in terms of a
business longevity perspective.

00:07:55.058 --> 00:07:56.880
- [Director] I need a raise.

00:07:56.880 --> 00:07:58.807
Three, two one.

00:08:04.563 --> 00:08:05.396
- Oh, man.

00:08:05.396 --> 00:08:06.276
I thought everyone was gonna go like,

00:08:06.276 --> 00:08:07.526
strongly agree.

00:08:09.988 --> 00:08:11.303
- We seem greedy.

00:08:11.303 --> 00:08:13.620
- I was thinking about going to disagree.

00:08:13.620 --> 00:08:14.453
But I mean,

00:08:14.453 --> 00:08:17.250
we all need a raise, right?

00:08:17.250 --> 00:08:18.120
- Yeah.

00:08:18.120 --> 00:08:19.634
- Can we all agree that like...

00:08:19.634 --> 00:08:22.603
- Yeah. Need is greater than want.

00:08:23.492 --> 00:08:25.290
Like want, I think a
lot of us would be here.

00:08:25.290 --> 00:08:26.310
But need is...

00:08:26.310 --> 00:08:27.633
- Okay.

00:08:27.633 --> 00:08:28.466
If we're going like desperately need,

00:08:28.466 --> 00:08:29.340
then I'm gonna go over here.

00:08:29.340 --> 00:08:30.700
- Well, I don't think it
should be desperately need.

00:08:30.700 --> 00:08:32.783
- Yeah. It depends on how you see it.

00:08:32.783 --> 00:08:34.620
I think that's how I perceive it.

00:08:34.620 --> 00:08:36.611
- Do you deserve a raise?

00:08:36.611 --> 00:08:37.444
I think is like,

00:08:37.444 --> 00:08:38.744
kind of...

00:08:38.744 --> 00:08:40.205
- I don't need a raise.

00:08:40.205 --> 00:08:41.940
- I think you should demand
more out of yourself.

00:08:41.940 --> 00:08:43.890
You work a lot of hours.

00:08:43.890 --> 00:08:45.210
On the weekends sometimes.

00:08:45.210 --> 00:08:46.470
Late into the night sometimes.

00:08:46.470 --> 00:08:48.210
- I think media companies generally pay

00:08:48.210 --> 00:08:49.530
their employees less.

00:08:49.530 --> 00:08:51.570
So if I compare to other media companies

00:08:51.570 --> 00:08:52.403
from whatever I've heard,

00:08:52.403 --> 00:08:54.900
I think we have a pretty decent salary.

00:08:54.900 --> 00:08:56.032
But yeah.

00:08:56.032 --> 00:08:57.120
If you compare to the
amount of work we put in.

00:08:57.120 --> 00:08:58.950
The hours we put in.

00:08:58.950 --> 00:09:00.120
Sometimes working weekends.

00:09:00.120 --> 00:09:03.180
And also a lot of times
the emotional labor

00:09:03.180 --> 00:09:05.917
that goes in the work
which is a lot of it.

00:09:05.917 --> 00:09:08.490
Especially for specific shows
or specific time of the year.

00:09:08.490 --> 00:09:10.830
And then I just think a
gradual raise would be good.

00:09:10.830 --> 00:09:13.350
I'm somebody who just
can't ask for a raise.

00:09:13.350 --> 00:09:15.720
And I think that's my flaw.

00:09:15.720 --> 00:09:17.190
That I should be more straightforward

00:09:17.190 --> 00:09:19.440
and should demand what I deserve.

00:09:19.440 --> 00:09:21.240
Because the companies run by people.

00:09:21.240 --> 00:09:23.130
And of course they'll try
to save money if they can.

00:09:23.130 --> 00:09:23.963
Right?

00:09:23.963 --> 00:09:24.843
- That's right.

00:09:24.843 --> 00:09:27.090
And it's actually true
that this year at any job,

00:09:27.090 --> 00:09:28.020
if you didn't get a raise,

00:09:28.020 --> 00:09:30.180
you got a pay cut because of inflation.

00:09:30.180 --> 00:09:32.310
So, even more reason.

00:09:32.310 --> 00:09:34.470
- I also think we're all in our twenties,

00:09:34.470 --> 00:09:35.303
and we are all single.

00:09:35.303 --> 00:09:36.570
Like, none of us are married right?

00:09:36.570 --> 00:09:39.630
So that's why we are more
in this range in the middle.

00:09:39.630 --> 00:09:41.850
I think if any of us had
responsibility of a child

00:09:41.850 --> 00:09:43.200
or had our own family

00:09:43.200 --> 00:09:44.938
we would be on the other side.

00:09:44.938 --> 00:09:45.771
- Yeah.

00:09:45.771 --> 00:09:46.953
- On the extreme side.

00:09:48.510 --> 00:09:51.930
- [Director] Our company
exists in a political bubble.

00:09:51.930 --> 00:09:54.180
Three, two, one.

00:10:00.659 --> 00:10:02.159
- This is so hard.

00:10:03.753 --> 00:10:04.586
- Oh, man.

00:10:07.590 --> 00:10:08.423
- As we mentioned like,

00:10:08.423 --> 00:10:09.256
we are all like,

00:10:09.256 --> 00:10:10.341
more left-leaning.

00:10:10.341 --> 00:10:12.300
But in the very first
spectrum Jubilee episode,

00:10:12.300 --> 00:10:15.450
we talked about how I feel
like important it was for like,

00:10:15.450 --> 00:10:17.070
us to hire a Trump supporter.

00:10:17.070 --> 00:10:19.530
- In terms of like thoughts
and opinions on things,

00:10:19.530 --> 00:10:21.870
I think we can be more diverse.

00:10:21.870 --> 00:10:23.220
- And we need a Trump voter,

00:10:23.220 --> 00:10:24.810
like you guys were saying.

00:10:24.810 --> 00:10:27.210
- And maybe we've done that since,

00:10:27.210 --> 00:10:28.110
maybe we haven't.

00:10:28.110 --> 00:10:29.250
But if we have,

00:10:29.250 --> 00:10:31.140
I don't think we've created an environment

00:10:31.140 --> 00:10:33.270
where that person feels comfortable enough

00:10:33.270 --> 00:10:35.647
to be vocal in our team about like,

00:10:35.647 --> 00:10:36.660
"Yes. I have supported Trump."

00:10:36.660 --> 00:10:38.310
Or, "Yes. I am more conservative."

00:10:38.310 --> 00:10:41.280
- Do you guys know single
conservative on our team?

00:10:41.280 --> 00:10:42.450
- I do know like,

00:10:42.450 --> 00:10:44.460
a current employee who's conservative only

00:10:44.460 --> 00:10:47.640
because of one of the shows
that I was involved in made

00:10:47.640 --> 00:10:50.610
so the staff had to talk to
us about this stuff privately.

00:10:50.610 --> 00:10:52.050
And they, you know,

00:10:52.050 --> 00:10:53.280
kind of made a joke about it

00:10:53.280 --> 00:10:54.577
and said something off the cuff like,

00:10:54.577 --> 00:10:55.410
"Oh. Well, you know,

00:10:55.410 --> 00:10:56.370
I'm kind of the black like,

00:10:56.370 --> 00:10:57.540
the secret black sheep."

00:10:57.540 --> 00:10:59.430
- I think we try our best to like,

00:10:59.430 --> 00:11:00.510
burst the bubble.

00:11:00.510 --> 00:11:01.440
And sometimes we do,

00:11:01.440 --> 00:11:03.720
but then we always catch
ourselves getting back

00:11:03.720 --> 00:11:06.390
into the bubble because
we are very left leaning.

00:11:06.390 --> 00:11:08.910
- I think it's really naive to even like,

00:11:08.910 --> 00:11:12.150
consider that we might actually like,

00:11:12.150 --> 00:11:15.900
not exist within the
political bubble at all.

00:11:15.900 --> 00:11:16.733
- Yeah.

00:11:16.733 --> 00:11:17.566
- Because like,

00:11:17.566 --> 00:11:19.530
even at the core of like,

00:11:19.530 --> 00:11:21.450
who we are as people like,

00:11:21.450 --> 00:11:24.420
all of our unconscious biases
are gonna affect the way

00:11:24.420 --> 00:11:25.253
that we work.

00:11:25.253 --> 00:11:28.170
And they're always gonna
affect the way that we interact

00:11:28.170 --> 00:11:29.940
with people and the ideas
that we come up with.

00:11:29.940 --> 00:11:31.230
- That's why I'm over here is

00:11:31.230 --> 00:11:32.970
because I know we're not exempt.

00:11:32.970 --> 00:11:33.870
You know?

00:11:33.870 --> 00:11:35.760
We see it in the content we make.

00:11:35.760 --> 00:11:37.590
- But a lot of our content is political.

00:11:37.590 --> 00:11:39.957
So I think that at that
point we get people

00:11:39.957 --> 00:11:41.070
who have different views.

00:11:41.070 --> 00:11:42.030
But inevitably,

00:11:42.030 --> 00:11:44.460
things would happen since
we're so left leaning

00:11:44.460 --> 00:11:46.380
that even if we accept someone,

00:11:46.380 --> 00:11:49.410
their beliefs might come in
the way of how we function.

00:11:49.410 --> 00:11:51.720
So maybe they wouldn't
work for the company.

00:11:51.720 --> 00:11:52.553
For example,

00:11:52.553 --> 00:11:54.510
I think most of us are
very anti-gun, right?

00:11:54.510 --> 00:11:56.670
But if we hire someone,
maybe who's pro-gun

00:11:56.670 --> 00:11:58.140
and brings a gun to the office

00:11:58.140 --> 00:11:59.850
because they need it for their safety,

00:11:59.850 --> 00:12:01.860
I think a lot of us will
have a problem with that.

00:12:01.860 --> 00:12:02.970
With someone bringing a gun.

00:12:02.970 --> 00:12:03.803
- Yeah.

00:12:03.803 --> 00:12:05.670
- So just by the nature of our work

00:12:05.670 --> 00:12:07.680
and the side we lean towards,

00:12:07.680 --> 00:12:09.153
I think we are in our bubble.

00:12:10.380 --> 00:12:14.490
- [Director] Jubilee will
still be around in 50 years.

00:12:14.490 --> 00:12:16.620
Three, two, one.

00:12:20.090 --> 00:12:22.203
- Oh dear. So we'll be like, 75-70.

00:12:24.164 --> 00:12:24.997
70's.

00:12:24.997 --> 00:12:26.567
Oh, no.

00:12:26.567 --> 00:12:28.036
- Oh, no!

00:12:28.036 --> 00:12:28.951
- Uh-oh.

00:12:28.951 --> 00:12:29.784
- Wow.

00:12:29.784 --> 00:12:31.440
- Uh-oh.

00:12:31.440 --> 00:12:34.530
I mean, I hope Jubilee is
still around in like 50 years.

00:12:34.530 --> 00:12:36.180
And I know like,

00:12:36.180 --> 00:12:38.340
Jason, our CEO, loves to talk about

00:12:38.340 --> 00:12:41.400
how Jubilee is a huge
movement for empathy.

00:12:41.400 --> 00:12:43.290
And he has like huge, huge ambitions.

00:12:43.290 --> 00:12:45.300
And us on YouTube is just like,

00:12:45.300 --> 00:12:48.223
the very, very start of this movement.

00:12:48.223 --> 00:12:49.056
I don't know.

00:12:49.056 --> 00:12:50.880
I guess sometimes it's just discouraging.

00:12:50.880 --> 00:12:54.806
Like, if you compare Jubilee
right now in terms of like,

00:12:54.806 --> 00:12:57.390
views and engagement with our audience.

00:12:57.390 --> 00:12:59.160
We have grown in subscribers.

00:12:59.160 --> 00:13:00.330
But at the same time,

00:13:00.330 --> 00:13:04.620
I feel like our engagement
has gone down a good amount.

00:13:04.620 --> 00:13:07.170
- The idea of Jubilee has only existed

00:13:07.170 --> 00:13:09.870
for a little more than 10 years by now.

00:13:09.870 --> 00:13:12.420
It's very hard to guess where we'll be,

00:13:12.420 --> 00:13:13.920
if we'll be in 50 years.

00:13:13.920 --> 00:13:15.870
I guess I'm just a little more optimistic.

00:13:15.870 --> 00:13:17.250
- Who's to say that like,

00:13:17.250 --> 00:13:19.170
where we are at is like,

00:13:19.170 --> 00:13:21.060
the best that we will ever be?

00:13:21.060 --> 00:13:24.570
- I mean, Disney 50 years
in was already making like,

00:13:24.570 --> 00:13:25.680
whatever, Snow White.

00:13:25.680 --> 00:13:27.930
Or whatever Disney movie came out 50 years

00:13:27.930 --> 00:13:29.700
after they started so...

00:13:29.700 --> 00:13:31.950
- Accepted is no less than Snow White.

00:13:31.950 --> 00:13:33.210
- Everyone watch Accepted.

00:13:33.210 --> 00:13:36.030
Streaming on Amazon Prime and...

00:13:36.030 --> 00:13:37.320
- Apple TV.
- That's right, Apple TV.

00:13:37.320 --> 00:13:39.383
- [Milky] 100% on rotten tomatoes.

00:13:39.383 --> 00:13:40.216
- [Brandon] 100%.

00:13:40.216 --> 00:13:41.310
Great documentary.

00:13:41.310 --> 00:13:42.180
- Specifically,

00:13:42.180 --> 00:13:44.730
I actually asked Jason
that exact question.

00:13:44.730 --> 00:13:47.400
If he thought Jubilee was
gonna be around for 50 years.

00:13:47.400 --> 00:13:50.070
He told me that he wanted
to be the Disney of empathy.

00:13:50.070 --> 00:13:51.228
And I was kind of like,

00:13:51.228 --> 00:13:52.320
(scoffs) look at this guy.

00:13:52.320 --> 00:13:53.730
He's gonna be Disney?

00:13:53.730 --> 00:13:54.930
But then I asked him like,

00:13:54.930 --> 00:13:59.700
how are you able to be so
ambitious and think that big?

00:13:59.700 --> 00:14:03.930
And he told me that one of
his friends who was just like,

00:14:03.930 --> 00:14:05.880
I think a coder in college,

00:14:05.880 --> 00:14:07.470
ended up creating Dropbox.

00:14:07.470 --> 00:14:09.210
Like, that's his personal friend.

00:14:09.210 --> 00:14:11.910
And because he saw his
friend able to do that,

00:14:11.910 --> 00:14:12.743
he just thought,

00:14:12.743 --> 00:14:13.576
"Well, if my friend can do that,

00:14:13.576 --> 00:14:14.409
why can't I do that?"

00:14:14.409 --> 00:14:16.050
And it gave me such a
valuable lesson about like,

00:14:16.050 --> 00:14:17.220
the types of people that you choose

00:14:17.220 --> 00:14:18.450
to surround yourself with.

00:14:18.450 --> 00:14:19.500
That once they rise up,

00:14:19.500 --> 00:14:20.333
you rise up too.

00:14:20.333 --> 00:14:22.290
So, that's why I'm very
thankful to be surrounded

00:14:22.290 --> 00:14:23.610
by such talented people, too.

00:14:23.610 --> 00:14:25.590
That I know will all be successful.

00:14:25.590 --> 00:14:27.030
So, like...

00:14:27.030 --> 00:14:27.900
If you guys are gonna do it,

00:14:27.900 --> 00:14:29.065
I'm gonna do it too.

00:14:29.065 --> 00:14:30.514
- (beep) Yeah.
- We'll all be here.

00:14:30.514 --> 00:14:31.347
- Yeah.

00:14:31.347 --> 00:14:32.180
- Empty here.

00:14:32.180 --> 00:14:33.870
- At Jubilee?

00:14:33.870 --> 00:14:35.520
- And we survived the pandemic also.

00:14:35.520 --> 00:14:36.353
Which was...

00:14:36.353 --> 00:14:37.500
Which would've really jolted the company,

00:14:37.500 --> 00:14:38.400
which it did in a way.

00:14:38.400 --> 00:14:40.410
But we still passed through that phase.

00:14:40.410 --> 00:14:42.450
And I think the mission of
Jubilee and what it stands

00:14:42.450 --> 00:14:46.050
for is just the ethos of the
company is here to stay on

00:14:46.050 --> 00:14:46.883
for pretty long.

00:14:46.883 --> 00:14:48.660
- Snaps to that. Right?

00:14:48.660 --> 00:14:49.847
- Wait, everyone.

00:14:49.847 --> 00:14:51.249
You gotta do it slightly
after the first person.

00:14:51.249 --> 00:14:52.749
- Three, two, one.

00:14:54.599 --> 00:14:55.432
Beautiful.

00:14:56.474 --> 00:14:57.307
- Now we're coming back up.

00:14:57.307 --> 00:14:58.743
And then the other side.

00:14:59.726 --> 00:15:01.380
Okay.

00:15:01.380 --> 00:15:02.332
- Does it look cool?

00:15:02.332 --> 00:15:03.165
- This is why we put nobody in front.

00:15:03.165 --> 00:15:04.500
- Does this look great?

00:15:04.500 --> 00:15:07.023
- I'm proud to be a citizen of my country.

00:15:07.980 --> 00:15:10.868
♪ I'm proud to be an American. ♪

